<p class="lede">The seed was a simple idea: a curated home for dance awards — one
    place where a family could find every result, and a studio owner could stop
    rebuilding the season from screenshots. This spring, the seed sprouted. And
    like most living things, it's growing into something I didn't fully plan.</p>

<p>The original promise was about making life easier. Families search once
    instead of digging through a dozen sites and vanished PDFs. Teachers stop
    hand-matching "1st Overall — Junior Small Group" to the right eight kids at
    midnight. A curated space, in the librarian's sense of the word: gathered,
    checked, organized, kept.</p>

<p>Then, while building the award pages, something unplanned happened. We kept
    asking: what <em>is</em> an award, really? Not the trophy — the moment. The
    routine, the teammates, the teacher in the wings, the parent who drove to
    every 6am call. A line in a results sheet holds none of that. So the award
    card started growing extra pages, like a small book: the certificate, a photo
    from that routine, and — the part I love most — room for
    <strong>thank-you notes</strong>, written by the dancers themselves, right
    there with the award.</p>

<p>As far as I know, nothing like this has existed before. Trophies tarnish and
    results pages disappear, but a thank-you note written at twelve — <em>thank
    you Coach Marina, and Mom for every 6am drive</em> — will read very
    differently at twenty. That's the part the seed idea never promised: we set
    out to organize records, and found ourselves keeping memories.</p>

<p>The plant is still small. But it's growing in a direction I trust: awards
    that stay, with the people and the gratitude that made them, attached for
    good. More soon.</p>
